  "cards": [
    {
      "dot": "cyan",
      "title": "The only thing Python cannot do",
      "items": [
        "Spawning lives in the session; a subprocess has no channel to it.",
        "The runner resolves the next phase and stops. It never spawns.",
        "The declared output existing and non-empty is the whole completion signal.",
        "No request file, no hash sidecar, no run id, no collector."
      ]
    },
    {
      "dot": "rose",
      "title": "Why no adapter was written",
      "items": [
        "The host offers agent(prompt, agent=..., handle=True).",
        "It is a builtin injected into the eval kernel only.",
        "python3 -c \"print(callable(agent))\" raises NameError.",
        "Every call site is bash-launched, so the gap is a process boundary."
      ]
    },
    {
      "dot": "amber",
      "title": "The trade, stated plainly",
      "items": [
        "A stale artifact reads as a finished phase.",
        "That is the simplest rule that can work, and it is the only gate there is.",
        "To redo a phase, delete its output file.",
        "A run manifest would remove the ambiguity and is the bookkeeping this design avoids."
      ]
    }
  ]
